he had wanted to rebuild his reputation  but after years denying the accusations  of abuse against him today any prospect  of a return to public life for the duke  of york is over in a swift and short  statement buckingham palace said that  with the queen's approval and agreement  the duke of york's military affiliations  and royal patronages have been returned  to the queen the duke of york it said  will continue not to undertake any  public duties and is defending this case  as a private citizen well this is an  unprecedented situation  the duke of york who is the queen's  second son  at one time he was second in line to the  throne  after his elder brother the prince of  wales and for 20 years  andrew held that position  um and this is very demeaning for him  but it was very necessary andrew knew  that something had to be done  and the queen knew that too  it was becoming increasingly untenable  for the duke to hold on to his remaining  royal roles i spoke to dozens of  organizations today that had links to  the duke all of them were keen to  distance themselves from him but none of  them were keen to speak on the record i  spoke to grenadier guards who said they  felt embarrassed by the duke's links to  their regiment ultimately though it was  the queen who made the decision about  his royal and military titles  seen leaving his home in windsor great  park today behind the scenes discussions  with the royal family and advisors have  been taking place an uncomfortable  balance for the queen as mother and  monarch the decision means that the duke  of york will lose his patronages and  affiliations to charities and  organizations like nhs trusts the royal  navy officers association and the sick  kids foundation and he loses military  titles including colonel of the  grenadier guards and colonel in chief of  the royal irish regiment he will no  longer be able to use his royal highness  in an official capacity but will remain  as a duke of york i certainly think it's  the correct decision for  uh prince andrew to give up his military  honorary titles such as  deputy colonel in chief of the royal  lancers  because he's now in a situation where  his integrity is in question  and his reputation  has been damaged so much  that the soldiers sailors and airmen  and air women who are  out on operations  cannot be comfortable  in  tasting him as they might have to do at  a dinner uh or indeed  if they had to  look up to him and speak to him  the army board will be looking at this  situation all the time with a view to  does he match the  standards and the values of the armed  forces in the 21st century  and i'm afraid  he doesn't match that standard  uh after what's happened in the last  week  the duke of york spent 22 years in the  royal navy and retained a number of  honorary military titles but he was  dogged for years by his relationship  with the late billionaire jeffrey  epstein and this was a decision to  protect the royal family from a damaging  distraction in the year of the platinum  jubilee the duke has always denied the  claims that he sexually abused virginia  du frey when she was a teenager a source  close to him said that this civil case  was a marathon and not a sprint and said  the duke will continue to defend himself  but today a clear line has been drawn  between the royal family and the queen's  second son ali fortescue sky news  well let's go straight to our royal  correspondent now ran on mills and  rhiannon it's been a dramatic turnaround  in this story in the past 24 hours what  do you read into the timing of the  palace's statement and who do you think  is behind this decision  oh you could not have had a more  dramatic change in the past 24 hours  from the palace saying they simply  weren't going to comment then this shock  statement effectively drawing a line  under prince andrew's royal role no  longer officially an hrh so who made the  decision in the end well the queen may  be head of the institution but i  understand that this was widely  discussed within the family the  statement itself is quite interesting it  doesn't make clear whether actually  prince andrew himself said that he would  hand over those titles again i suspect  that's unlikely one thing i suspect is  likely is that prince charles and prince  william were probably heavily involved  in this decision after all they are  increasingly taking on roles on behalf  the queen and they are just as invested  as she is when it comes to trying to  protect the raw name also within that  statement i think a very telling  sentence where it says he's now going to  defend his name as a private citizen  again i think forcing him out into the  cold in many ways saying that actually  it's time for him to face this as an  individual and not as a royal as we had  a potential trial at the end of the year  also though i think why now why do this  now because yes of course the judge's  decision yesterday was very significant  but the allegations against prince  andrew have been the same for a number  of years but i get the sense that within  the palace there was a belief that  actually the fact they weren't acting  was going to be more damaging in the  long run you would not want a situation  where the queen for example was having  to be advised by her prime minister or  by the cabinet that they had to that she  had to act on behalf of what her son was  doing maybe to stop any international  embarrassment and also of course don't  forget this was always going to be a  year where the royal family were going  to be in the spotlight i think they're  hoping that this will in some ways draw  a line under things as they head towards  the platinum jubilee but i think sadly  for them a story of this magnitude  is not going to go away and will  continue to cloud the run-up to those  celebrations whether they like it or not
 

